   "etapa": "explicar",
   "piezas": [
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "Event, background and present",
     "texto": [
      "In a story, each past has a job:\n\n- The **indefinido** moves the story forward: **Llegué a las ocho.**\n- The **imperfect** describes the background, the habit, or what was in progress: **Llovía.**\n- The **perfecto** links a period or an experience with today: **Esta semana he leído dos libros.**\n\nThis last choice changes by region: some people use the indefinido where others use the perfecto. The examples here follow a common contrast in Spain, not a rule for everyone.\n\nLook for clues: **ayer, de repente** (indefinido), **antes, siempre, de pequeño** (imperfect), **esta semana, desde entonces** (perfecto)."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "gramatica",
     "titulo": "Three tenses, three jobs",
     "texto": [
      "An easy way to choose:\n\n- Does it tell **an event** that moves the story forward? **Indefinido**: **llegué, esperé, salió**.\n- Does it describe **what something was like**, **a habit**, or what **was going on**? **Imperfect**: **llovía, iba, era**.\n- Does it connect with **today** or with a period that includes today? **Perfecto**: **he vuelto, he pensado**.\n\nThey can live in the same sentence: **Llovía cuando llegué.** (background + event)\n\nRemember that the perfecto and the indefinido are shared out differently in different regions. Both forms are understood everywhere."
     ],
